@title Two Minutes at the Poster
@kind paper
@source paper:Obfuscation as a Probe of Human and Machine Code Comprehension
@cite J. V. Le (2026). Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do? Obfuscation as a Probe of Human and Machine Code Comprehension. ACM Student Research Competition, SPLASH/ISSTA. Two-page extended abstract; peer-reviewed.
@link ../../assets/documents/publications/2026SPLASH-SRC.pdf Read the two pages
@link https://doi.org/10.1145/3837729.3839573 DOI
@authors J. V. Le
@note The frame story is fiction: the hall, the corridor, the poster, the two-minute clock, every question the Judge asks, and the night of cutting. The Judge is an abstract role, not a real competition judge, and the Two Pages is the abstract given a voice. The "guess" that is cut is invented and never stated; the story reports nothing that is not printed on the two pages. The human and model studies the abstract summarizes are joint work, credited in their own stories; no coauthor is voiced. Only lines marked § or p. report the abstract.
@status published
@verify
@palette ochre
@include _facts/obfuscation.vn
@cast Jack hue=210 skin=2 glasses short masc shirt
@cast Judge name="the Judge" hue=350 skin=3 glasses bun fem uniform
@cast Page page name="the Two Pages"

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1

@chapter 1 The Form
@transition fade 900
@bg corridor
@mode nvl
There is a kind of writing that is mostly subtraction.
Mine is called an extended abstract. Extended, here, means two pages. ^p.1
Two pages hold a title, an abstract, four numbered sections, one figure, and {n_refs} references.
Everything else I know about the work is somewhere else. Today it may as well not exist.
@mode adv
@show Jack center (thinking)
A corridor in a convention centre, mid-morning. Carpet chosen by a committee. Daylight doing its best.
Jack: Two pages on the easel. Two minutes per judge.
Jack (deadpan): I have spent longer than that choosing a font size for a caption.
@transition dissolve 700
@cg page | Two pages
The title asks a question. *Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do?* ^p.1
The subtitle answers a different one: what the work is for. *Obfuscation as a probe of human and machine code comprehension.* ^p.1
@cg off
@show Jack center (neutral)
Jack: Two minutes has room for one opening. Which?
* The question in the title. -> open_question
* The result nobody expected. -> open_surprise

== open_question
@set lead = question
Jack (smile): Ask it straight. If the question is good, the judge does half the talking.
-> ch1_out

== open_surprise
@set lead = surprise
Jack (smile): Start where the eyebrows go up. Then walk back to why anyone looked.
-> ch1_out

== ch1_out
@move Jack right
(Whichever I choose, the pages say the same thing. That is the point of pages.)
@hide Jack
@pause 400

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2

@chapter 2 Two Minutes
@transition wipe-left 800
@bg lecture
@fx dust on
They have given the posters a lecture hall. The easels stand in a row at the front, where a lecturer would pace.
@show Jack left (neutral)
Mine is the third from the door. I have straightened it twice. It was straight both times.
@show Judge right far
Someone with a clipboard is working down the row. They stop at each easel for exactly as long as they mean to.
@show Judge right (deadpan)
Judge: Two minutes. I have read the two pages. Tell me what they are for.
@if lead == surprise -> lead_surprise
Jack: A question, then. When code is made hard to read on purpose, do machines get lost where people do?
Judge (neutral): That is your title with the capitals taken off.
-> pitch

== lead_surprise
Jack: The surprise, then. It is on the second page, and it is in Python.
Judge (puzzled): You will get there. First tell me why anyone looked.
-> pitch

== pitch
Jack (neutral): Code obfuscation targets human comprehension, but current evaluations primarily measure language model performance
  degradation instead of whether models fail in the same way as humans. ^§abs
Judge (thinking): So everyone measures the machine, and nobody asks the person.
Jack: Obfuscation is almost always measured without people, on two untested assumptions: that difficulty is monotonic,
  and that what burdens a person burdens an automated analyst. ^§1
Jack: If models fail where humans do, obfuscated code benchmarks human-like reasoning. If not, strong scores mask brittle competence. ^§1
Judge (neutral): What did you do to the code?
@card Figure 1, the only figure on the two pages | L0: the original function with its name masked | L1: uninformative renaming | L1b: adversarial renaming | L2: control-flow flattening | L3: both, an upper bound on difficulty ^§2.1
Jack: Five tiers, each verified execution-equivalent on the study's inputs. ^§2.1
Judge: Show me the unkind one.
@transition dissolve 500
@cg screen-code | Illustrative only; the abstract prints the name, not the function
Jack: L1b. Like a Stroop task: a variable named `_lastNSecs` that tracks recursion depth. ^§2.2
Jack: It baits a fast, confident, wrong answer. ^§2.2
@cg off
Judge (smile): I was about to say seconds.
Jack (smile): That is the trap doing its job.
Judge (neutral): Who read it?
Jack (neutral): {n_humans} undergraduate CS majors, a supervised {session_min}-minute session of {n_questions} questions. Output prediction, scored by exact match
  against execution: {n_answers} responses with paired correctness and response time.
Jack: {n_models} language models, {model_sizes}, on the same tiers. Of those, {n_models_human} enter the human comparison. {n_runs} runs.
@show Judge right (deadpan) near
@thumb
Judge: That was a minute. You have four contributions and the other one.
Jack (worried): In order?
Judge: In any order. I will stop you when I stop caring.
@set told = 0

== hub
* (once) One: the correlation. -> c_one
* (once) Two: Python. -> c_two
* (once) Three: which models. -> c_three
* (once) Four: the one that reads like a form. -> c_four
* (if told == 4) That is all four. -> hub_done

== c_one
@add told 1
Jack (neutral): One. The first per-task human–model difficulty correlation, localized via the Block Model. ^§4
Judge (thinking): Per task. Most people bring me an average.
Jack: Reasoning-tuned models correlate with human task-level difficulty. Spearman {rho_reason}.
Judge (neutral): And localized means?
Jack: Schulte's Block Model: atoms, blocks, relations, macro structure. ^§2.3
Jack: Chain-of-thought length correlates negatively with accuracy, {rho_cot}. Control-flow failure tracks dispatcher-state complexity:
  the relational level.
Jack: Adversarial renaming acts non-additively at the atom level. Displacement or perplexity spikes alone barely move accuracy;
  together they collapse it to {adv_collapse}.
Judge (deadpan): I asked where it breaks and you handed me a floor plan. Fine.
-> hub

== c_two
@add told 1
Jack (neutral): Two. Evidence against monotonic difficulty: a reproducible Python renaming effect. ^§4
Judge (puzzled): Monotonic meaning: more obfuscation, worse reading.
Jack: From a distance it holds. Human accuracy falls from {human_l0} at L0 to {human_l3} at L3.
Jack: JavaScript declines steadily, {js_l0} to {js_l3}, flattening hurting less than renaming. ^§4
Jack (smile): Python rises under renaming. {py_l0} to {py_rename}. And L3 exceeds L2. ^§4
Judge (surprised): Rises.
Judge (thinking): Why?
Jack (deadpan): That is a longer answer than the other three put together.
Judge (deadpan): Then it can wait behind them.
-> hub

== c_three
@add told 1
Jack (neutral): Three. Only reasoning-tuned models track human difficulty. ^§4
Jack: Coder- and instruction-tuned models do not, {rho_other}, regardless of size.
Judge (puzzled): Not even the ones that score best?
Jack: The most accurate model is not the most human-like. ^§4
Jack: And the alignment holds across all {n_bands} experience bands, {rho_bands}.
Judge (thinking): So the model reads like a person.
Jack (worried): No. No model reproduces the experts' gain under adversarial renaming, or the Python renaming gain.
  Alignment reflects shared difficulty, not identical mechanism. ^§4
Judge (smile): You have just talked me out of your own headline.
Jack (smile): The headline ends in a question mark. ^p.1
-> hub

== c_four
@add told 1
Jack (neutral): Four. Language-specific behavioral reporting of obfuscation strength. ^§4
Judge (deadpan): That one is a noun phrase wearing a lanyard.
Jack (laugh): It had one line to live on.
Jack (neutral): Difficulty is non-monotonic and language-specific. The same five tiers went one way in JavaScript and another in Python. ^§4
Jack: And behavioral includes the clock. Response time jumps by {rt_jump} once code is obfuscated, then plateaus;
  errors cluster at time extremes, and correct L1b answers are slower than incorrect ones.
Judge (thinking): So "how strong is this obfuscation" is the wrong shape of question.
Jack: Unless it says in which language, and to whom.
-> hub

== hub_done
@show Judge right (thinking) near
Judge: Four. Now the one you put off. Python rises. Why?
Jack (worried): The two pages do not say.
Judge (deadpan): Because you do not know, or because it did not fit?
@pause 700
(There is an honest answer to that. It is a night in June.)
@hide all
@fx dust off

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3

@chapter 3 What Was Cut
@fx flash
@flashback on June
@transition white 800
@cg desk-night | The template on the left, everything else on the right
The template was open on the left. Everything I wanted to say was open on the right.
The template had already decided the length. I was only negotiating the terms.
@transition dissolve 700
@cg off
@bg room
@show Page center
Page: I am two pages. I was two pages before you sat down and I will be two pages when you get up.
@show Jack left (worried)
@move Page right
Jack: I have a paragraph about why Python does that.
Page: Is it a result?
Jack (thinking): It is a guess. A good one.
Page: I print what was measured. A guess wants more room than I have, and then it wants a control.
* Cut the guess. -> cut_guess
* Cut something else, and keep the guess. -> cut_other

== cut_guess
Jack (deadpan): Gone.
It went quietly. Good guesses do; they expect to be invited back.
-> cut_join

== cut_other
Jack (thinking): An adjective first. Then another.
Page: I had noticed you were fond of them.
Somewhere around the hour the adjectives ran out, the guess went too.
-> cut_join

== cut_join
@hide all
@transition dissolve 600
@cg hands-keyboard | Select. Delete.
@mode nvl
What stayed is what two pages can stand behind. The effect itself, and one careful word for it: reproducible. ^§4
A whole model study survives as a parenthesis: {n_runs} runs.
{n_refs} references made it. One of them is from {stroop_year}, and it earns its line: the trap is named after it. ^§2.2
@page
I had thought of the form as a smaller paper. It is not. It is a claim about which sentences could not be removed.
Every sentence still on the page is there because cutting it broke something.
That is the nearest thing to a proof a writer gets.
@mode adv
@pause 500
@cg off
@flashback off

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4

@chapter 4 Time
@transition dissolve 800
@bg lecture
@fx dust on
@show Jack left (neutral)
@show Judge right (deadpan) near
Jack: The two pages do not say why. They say the effect is reproducible, and that it is evidence against monotonic difficulty. ^§4
Jack (thinking): I would rather hand you an effect without a story than a story without an effect.
Judge (smile): That is the first thing you have said that is not on the poster.
Judge (neutral): Time. One sentence for the clipboard. Choose it.
* The one about alignment. -> last_align
* The one with the question mark. -> last_question

== last_align
Jack (thinking): The last sentence of the abstract. It was written to be the one that survives.
-> last_join

== last_question
Jack (smile): Do machines struggle where humans do.
Judge (deadpan): That is a question. I asked for a sentence.
Jack (neutral): Then the sentence that answers it.
-> last_join

== last_join
Jack (neutral): Only reasoning-tuned models mirror human difficulty patterns. This alignment, rather than raw accuracy,
  distinguishes robust comprehension from brittle pattern matching. ^§abs
The Judge writes for longer than the sentence is long.
Judge (deadpan): That was two minutes and some. I will not tell anyone.
@show Judge right (smile) far
Judge: Straighten your poster.
@hide Judge
Jack (puzzled): It is straight.
@move Jack center
@pause 500
@card What the two pages claim | 1: The first per-task human–model difficulty correlation, localized via the Block Model | 2: Evidence against monotonic difficulty: a reproducible Python renaming effect | 3: Only reasoning-tuned models track human difficulty | 4: Language-specific behavioral reporting of obfuscation strength ^§4
@hide Jack
@fx dust off
@transition dissolve 900
@cg corridor-light | The last of the afternoon
The judges come and go until the windows change colour. I say my two minutes until they stop sounding like mine.
The bottom of the second page carries two dates in small type. Received {received}; accepted {accepted}.
Two pages do not get any longer while they wait. It is their one great virtue.
@transition dissolve 700
@bg corridor dusk
@cg off
@tone dusk
@show Jack center (smile)
Jack: Two pages. They fit in a jacket pocket.
(I straighten the poster one last time, and take it down.)
@pause 500
@tone none
@end
